  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Southwesterly winds will develop and strengthen \nthrough this evening, then peak tonight-Fri. \n\nThe models indicate an intense mid/upr-level cyclone, now making an \nenewd pivot across the TN Valley in GOES-E satellite data, will \ncontinue to deepen while tracking near or just west of the spine of \nthe cntl Appalachians through Fri. It will be preceded by intense, \n150-200 meter 500 mb height falls and a strong, tropospheric-deep \nfrontal zone that will wrap around it and across the cntl NC this \nevening. \n\nAt the surface, a 996 mb low over Tidewater VA --one which tracked \nfarther east than what most model guidance indicated as it came \nashore near ILM between 09-10Z and subsequently tracked to near ETC \nand AKQ--  will continue nnewd near the coast of the Middle Atlantic \nand New England through early Friday. During the same time, a \nseparate low will deepen and occlude across the OH Valley and lwr \nGreat Lakes, beneath the center of the intense mid/upr feature noted \nabove. The surface reflection of the troposhperic-deep frontal zone \nwas analyzed near the 48-50 F isodrosotherm from ern TN through nrn \nand ern GA and marked the leading edge of abrupt drying and cooling \nmid afternoon temperatures. As that feature progresses newd and \nacross the Carolinas through this evening, post-frontal wind gusts, \nalready-noteworthy in the mid 20s to lwr 30s kts observed upstream \nacross cntl AL/GA, will strengthen. Indeed, point forecast soundings \nacross cntl NC depict swly momentum markedly increasing to 35-40 kts \nat the top of a boundary layer well-mixed by CAA along and behind \nthe front tonight. While most surface wind gusts will likely hold \naround the lwr end of that range (ie. 30-35 kts) and continue into \nFri, isolated gusts to around the upr end (ie. 40 kts) will be \npossible, especially between midnight and noon Fri. If it becomes \napparent that those upr end wind gusts are materializing upstream \nfrom GSP to CLT in the few hours centered around 00Z, a short-fused \nWind Advisory may be needed for cntl NC. Regardless, some additional \npower outages will probably result, particularly if instances of \nuprooting of trees result from the combination of rain-soaked soil \nand those winds, which will be 180 degrees opposite of the prolonged \nnely buffeting experienced Wed night-this morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... It will be briefly milder this weekend, with the \nnext chance for rain late Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nAloft, a nrn stream s/w, which is expected to move onshore over the \nPacific Northwest Fri/Fri night, will track esewd across the nrn \nRockies and cntl Plains through Sat. The s/w trough will amplify as \nit continues ewd across the MS, OH, and TN Valley regions Sat night \nand Sun, perhaps taking a slight negative tilt as it moves across \nthe mid-Atlantic and Northeast late Sun into early Mon, before \nshifting offshore on Mon. At the surface, a low pressure system will \ndevelop off the Rockies and move across the Plains Sat/Sat night, \nwhile high pressure moves ewd across the nrn Gulf and FL. The low \nwill then track ewd-newd across the Midwest and OH Valley on Sun and \nthe ern Great Lakes and Northeast Sun night/Mon. Meanwhile, a warm \nfront should lift across cntl NC immediately ahead of the attendant \ncold front, which should move ewd across cntl NC late Sun/Sun night. \nIf the parent low begins to occlude as it tracks across the OH \nValley/ern Great Lakes, a secondary triple point low may develop \neast of the Appalachians over wrn VA/NC, but it is too early to have \nvery high confidence in that development. There also appears to be \nperhaps a brief window of some higher theta-e advection into the \narea Sun evening. Instability still appears minimal, however the \nwind/shear profiles remain robust, with a rapidly strengthening low \nand mid-level jet over the area as the base of the trough swings \nacross. The 12Z operational GFS has 50-60 kts at 925 mb and 70-80 \nkts at 850 mb over the eastern half to two-thirds of cntl NC around \n06Z Mon. PWATs should also increase briefly to 1.2 to 1.5 inches NW \nto SE ahead of the cold front. For now, there is still a high \nprobability for showers and perhaps some storms, along and ahead of \nthe front Sun eve through Sun night/early Mon. It may also be quite \nbreezy, with the potential for some strong gusts if any deeper \nconvection is able to develop, but for now there is too much \nuncertainty/low confidence for inclusion in any outlooks.\n\nTemperatures: This weekend should be mild, with highs in the upper \n50s to mid 60s, and lows Sun night, based on current fropa timing, \nmay be well above normal by some 10-15 degrees (mid 40s to low 50s \ndegrees).",
